<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Southern Tang</h1><p class="st-meta">937 CE – 976 CE · capital Jinling</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest and most cultured of the southern Ten Kingdoms, ruled from Nanjing by a house that claimed Tang descent and ended with the poet-king Li Yu.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bian">Xu Zhigao</a>, adopted son of the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-wen">Xu Wen</a>, took the throne of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wu-ten-kingdoms">Wu (Ten Kingdoms)</a> in 937. In 939 he took the name Li Bian, claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> imperial family and renamed his state Tang, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Jinling</a> (Nanjing).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> held the wealthy lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jing">Li Jing</a> it conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/min-ten-kingdoms">Min (Ten Kingdoms)</a> in 945 and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ma-chu">Ma Chu</a> in 951 but could not hold them. Its court patronised painting, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a> and lyric poetry, and its paper and ink were famous. In 958 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a> took its lands between the Huai and Yangtze, and it became a tributary of the north.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yu-southern-tang">Li Yu</a>, a celebrated writer of song lyrics, tried to appease the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> armies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Jinling</a> in 975. He was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, where his poems lamenting his lost kingdom were composed, and died in 978, reputedly poisoned.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/southern-tang.jpg" alt="Southern Tang" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Later_Zhou.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>937 CE – 976 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jinling</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Li Bian, Li Jing, Li Yu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nan Tang, Jiangnan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=956&amp;focus=southern-tang" aria-label="Southern Tang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7736 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Southern Tang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8056 1267L8068 1256M8056 1267L7901 1174M8056 1267L8151 1476M8056 1267L7959 1401"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8068 1256h0M7901 1174h0M8151 1476h0M7959 1401h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8056 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8056 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=956&amp;focus=southern-tang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
